// Forty additional practical guides, each paired with a live tool route.
const guides = [
  ["how-to-convert-word-to-pdf","How to Convert Word to PDF Without Breaking the Layout","Convert a DOCX file to PDF and check fonts, page breaks, links, and privacy before sharing.","word-to-pdf","A Word document can reflow when it opens on another device because fonts, page size, and software versions differ. PDF preserves a page-oriented view for printing and sharing, but only when the conversion includes the content and layout you intend. Start with a copy of the source file and decide whether comments, tracked changes, and document properties should appear.","Open the DOCX and accept or reject tracked changes deliberately. Check embedded fonts and page size, then convert the file. Compare the first page, a page with a table, and the final page. Test hyperlinks and confirm that headers, footers, and page numbers remain in place.","A PDF is a distribution copy, not a replacement for the editable original. Complex fonts or unsupported embedded objects may render differently. For confidential material, choose a conversion path you trust and inspect the final file for comments, hidden text, or metadata you did not intend to share.","A good final check compares page count, headings, tables, links, and print preview against the approved DOCX. Keep both versions with clear filenames so later edits do not get lost.","Does Word to PDF preserve formatting?","It usually preserves page layout well, but fonts, unsupported objects, comments, and print settings can affect the result. Always inspect the converted pages.","https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/save-or-convert-to-pdf-or-xps-in-word')"],
  ["how-to-convert-pdf-to-word","How to Convert PDF to Word for Editable Text","Turn a PDF into an editable Word document and understand OCR, layout, and table limitations.","pdf-to-word","PDF is designed to present pages consistently, while Word is designed for editing flowing text. Conversion therefore reconstructs paragraphs, columns, and tables from page geometry. Text-based PDFs usually convert more cleanly than scans; scanned pages need optical character recognition (OCR), which can misread low-resolution text.","Check whether you can select and copy text in the source PDF. If not, use OCR and verify names, numbers, and punctuation manually. Convert a representative page first, then inspect headings, columns, tables, footnotes, and page breaks. Rebuild a complex table when preserving its visual layout creates awkward editing.","Do not treat a converted document as a verified transcript. OCR can confuse similar characters such as O and 0, while multi-column reading order can be wrong. Keep the original PDF and use a protected local workflow for sensitive documents. A scan’s resolution and contrast influence recognition quality.","For a two-column report, compare the extracted text order with the original page before editing. Correct the document structure in Word rather than adding spaces to imitate the PDF layout.","Can every PDF be converted to editable Word text?","No. Scanned pages require OCR, and intricate layouts may need manual cleanup. The original PDF remains the reference for checking content.","https://helpx.adobe.com/acrobat/using/exporting-pdfs-file-formats.html')"],
  ["how-to-convert-jpg-to-pdf","How to Convert JPG to PDF for Sharing or Printing","Combine JPG photos into a PDF, set page order, and check orientation and print size.","jpg-to-pdf","A PDF is useful when several photos need to travel as one attachment or print as a predictable set of pages. Before conversion, sort images in the order they should appear and decide whether each photo should fill a page or sit within margins. Camera images can have different orientations and aspect ratios.","Choose the images and review their sequence in a thumbnail view. Rotate any sideways photo before export, select a page size that matches the intended use, and choose fit or fill deliberately. Open the finished PDF and inspect every page, especially the edges of landscape photos and any image with text.","Filling the page can crop parts of a photo; fitting the whole image can leave blank margins. Large camera files can create a bulky PDF, so compress copies when email size matters. Keep the originals if image quality or later editing is important.","A set of six portrait receipts may be easier to review as one image per letter-size page with consistent margins than as a tightly cropped collage.","Will JPG to PDF reduce photo quality?","It can, depending on the export settings. Review the PDF at normal size and use a suitable quality setting for printing or screen sharing.","https://helpx.adobe.com/acrobat/using/creating-simple-pdfs-acrobat.html')"],
  ["how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg","How to Convert PDF Pages to JPG Images","Export PDF pages as JPGs for previews and sharing while choosing a suitable resolution.","pdf-to-image","A JPG image is convenient for previews, messages, and platforms that do not accept PDF uploads. Each PDF page becomes an image, so text is no longer selectable and file size depends on page dimensions and export resolution. Decide whether the result is for a small preview or a readable full-page image.","Select the pages you need and choose a resolution appropriate to the destination. Open a sample output and zoom in on small text, diagrams, and thin lines. Check that page orientation and crop boundaries are correct. If a document has many pages, export only the relevant range to avoid a large folder.","JPG uses lossy compression, so repeated editing and saving can introduce artifacts. For diagrams with sharp edges or text, PNG may look cleaner. Do not publish a screenshot of a document containing private details; image conversion does not remove the information from view.","For an email preview, a moderate image resolution is usually enough; for printing, use a higher resolution and verify the physical page size.","Can I convert only one PDF page to JPG?","Yes. Select the page or page range before exporting, then confirm the output image matches the required resolution and orientation.","https://helpx.adobe.com/acrobat/using/exporting-pdfs-file-formats.html')"],
  ["how-to-compress-images-for-email","How to Compress Images for Email Without Oversizing","Reduce image file size for email by adjusting dimensions and format while keeping a clear copy.","image-compressor","Email attachments often fail because a phone photo is far larger than the message needs. Reduce pixel dimensions to the size recipients will view, then choose a format that fits the image. Keep the untouched original so compression can be repeated with different settings.","Check the attachment limit of the mail service and leave room for the email’s encoding overhead. Resize a copy to the longest dimension needed on screen, then compare a compressed preview at actual viewing size. Photographs generally suit JPG or WebP; screenshots with small text may need PNG or a higher-quality setting.","Compression does not guarantee a message will fit if it contains many images or other attachments. Avoid compressing an image repeatedly because each lossy save can degrade detail. Use a secure sharing method for sensitive images instead of sending them as ordinary attachments.","If a recipient only needs to view a photo on a phone, an image around the display width is more practical than the original multi-megapixel camera file.","How do I make a photo small enough to email?","Resize a copy to the recipient’s viewing dimensions, compress it, and check the final file size against the provider’s attachment limit.","https://support.google.com/mail/answer/6584')"],
  ["how-to-resize-images-for-instagram","How to Resize Images for Instagram Without Cropping Faces","Prepare image dimensions for Instagram posts and stories while keeping important content inside safe areas.","aspect-ratio-resizer","A platform’s image shape affects how a photo appears in a feed, profile grid, and story. Resizing changes pixel dimensions; cropping changes what remains visible. Start from a high-quality original and select the destination format before editing so faces, labels, and text stay within the visible frame.","Choose the target placement, then use its current aspect-ratio guidance. Preview the crop at phone size and keep important subjects away from edges that may be clipped in previews. If a square crop is required, add background space rather than stretching the photo. Export a copy at a suitable resolution.","Social platforms can change upload specifications and may recompress images. Avoid tiny text near the edge, and do not assume one export works equally well for feed, story, and profile thumbnails. Check the live preview in the destination app before publishing.","A portrait photo can be padded into a square canvas for a profile grid preview, preserving the full image instead of cutting off a person’s head.","Should I stretch a photo to fit a social post?","No. Preserve the aspect ratio and crop or add canvas space; stretching distorts faces, products, and text.","https://help.instagram.com/')"],
  ["how-to-remove-background-from-image","How to Remove an Image Background Cleanly","Create a transparent background and inspect edges around hair, glass, and fine details.","image-background-remover","Removing a background works best when the subject contrasts with what is behind it. A transparent PNG keeps the cutout reusable over new colors or layouts. Choose a source image with enough resolution for the final use, and save an untouched copy before editing.","Run the removal, then inspect the silhouette against both light and dark preview colors. Zoom in around hair, transparent objects, and shadows; refine edge spill or missing details where controls are available. Export as PNG when transparency is required and confirm the background actually appears transparent in another viewer.","JPG cannot store transparency and may replace a transparent area with a solid color. Fine hair, motion blur, reflective glass, and similar colors at the subject boundary can produce imperfect edges. Do not use a cutout to misrepresent a product or person.","For a product photo, review the bottom edge and cast shadow as carefully as the outline; an unnaturally sharp cutout can look detached from its new background.","What file format keeps a removed background transparent?","PNG supports transparency in common workflows. Check the exported file on a contrasting background to confirm the alpha channel is present.","https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Media/Formats/Image_types')"],
  ["how-to-create-a-favicon-from-image","How to Create a Favicon from a Logo or Image","Generate favicon sizes from a clean source image and add the files to a website correctly.","favicon-from-image","A favicon appears in browser tabs, bookmarks, and shortcuts, where it may be displayed at a very small size. Start with a simple square logo or symbol and make sure it remains recognizable when reduced. A detailed wordmark usually needs a simplified mark for tiny displays.","Crop the source to a balanced square, leave a little padding, and generate the sizes your site needs. Preview the smallest size at actual pixel dimensions rather than relying on a zoomed image. Add the generated files and link tags to the site head, then test in a browser after clearing its cached icon.","An image can be technically valid but unreadable when reduced. Transparent edges may disappear on some tab backgrounds, so check contrast in light and dark browser themes. Keep original artwork and use a copyright-cleared logo.","A distinct letterform or geometric mark generally survives a 16-pixel tab icon better than a full business name in tiny text.","Which image is best for a favicon?","Use a clear square mark with strong contrast and minimal detail, then verify it at the smallest intended display size.","https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Favicon')"],
  ["how-to-protect-pdf-with-password","How to Protect a PDF with a Password","Understand PDF passwords, encryption compatibility, and safe ways to share protected files.","pdf-password-protector","PDF security settings can restrict opening or editing, but the protection depends on the encryption method and how the password is shared. Decide whether recipients need a password to open the file or whether you are only limiting actions such as printing. Keep an accessible original in a secure location.","Choose a strong, unique password and check the document in a separate PDF viewer after applying protection. Confirm that the intended restriction is active and that authorized recipients can still read the content. Share the password through a separate trusted channel rather than in the same email as the attachment.","A password cannot prevent an authorized recipient from photographing or copying visible content, and weak passwords may be guessed. Do not use PDF permissions as a substitute for access control on highly sensitive records. Avoid uploading confidential files to services you have not approved.","Send the protected PDF as one message and its password through a separate channel; test the recipient workflow using a non-sensitive sample first.","Does a PDF password make a file completely secure?","No. It adds a layer of protection, but security depends on encryption, password strength, handling, and recipient access.","https://www.adobe.com/acrobat/resources/document-security.html')"],
  ["how-to-add-page-numbers-to-pdf","How to Add Page Numbers to a PDF","Add page numbers to a PDF and check placement, numbering starts, and printed margins.","pdf-page-numbers","Page numbers help readers reference long PDFs, but they can obscure content if placed too close to the page edge. Decide whether the cover should be numbered, whether numbering starts at one or a later value, and whether the number should match the printed page or document section.","Use a copy of the PDF and choose a position with clear space on every page. Preview pages with different layouts, including a landscape page and one with a footer. If the cover is unnumbered, check the first visible number and verify the sequence through the final page.","A PDF may contain scanned pages, mixed sizes, or existing footer text. These can make one global placement unsuitable. Check the result in print preview and retain an unnumbered source so you can correct placement without layering duplicate numbers.","For a report whose cover is excluded, set the visible first page number to 1 on the second PDF page, then inspect the sequence at the end.","Can PDF page numbering start after the cover?","Yes, if the numbering tool supports an offset or starting-page option. Verify the displayed sequence on the first and last pages.","https://helpx.adobe.com/acrobat/using/add-headers-footers-pdfs.html')"],
  ["how-to-rotate-pdf-pages","How to Rotate PDF Pages Permanently","Correct sideways PDF pages and confirm rotation is saved rather than only changed in a viewer.","pdf-rotate","A viewer’s rotate button may change only the current display, while a page rotation operation should be saved into the PDF. Check whether the whole document or selected pages need rotation. Mixed portrait and landscape pages are common in reports assembled from scans and exports.","Make a copy, select the sideways pages, and rotate them in the correct direction. Save the result and close and reopen it to confirm the change persists. Check text orientation, page order, and any page labels after rotating. A landscape page may be intentional, so inspect before applying a bulk action.","Rotation does not straighten a photograph that was scanned at an angle, and it may not change the underlying image pixels. If a page remains crooked, deskew or rescan it. Avoid rotating all pages when only a few are sideways.","After correcting pages 3 and 8, reopen the saved file and verify that pages 2, 3, 4, 7, 8, and 9 all read in the intended orientation.","Why does a PDF page rotate back after I close it?","The viewer may have changed only its display state. Save a permanent page rotation and reopen the document to verify it.","https://helpx.adobe.com/acrobat/using/rotate-move-delete-crop-pdf-pages.html')"],
  ["how-to-create-pdf-from-multiple-images","How to Create a PDF from Multiple Images","Arrange images into a multi-page PDF with consistent orientation, page size, and readable quality.","image-to-pdf","Combining images into a PDF is helpful for receipts, scanned forms, and photo records. Sort files before import because filename order may not match the intended reading order. Decide whether each image should use a full page or fit within margins, and account for different image proportions.","Rename or reorder images into the sequence you need, then preview thumbnails before export. Select a page size and orientation that suit the source. Inspect one page with small text and one page with a wide image. Reopen the PDF to confirm all pages are present and the file size is practical.","A full-page crop can cut off document edges, while fit-to-page can leave margins. High-resolution scans may create a large file; compressing too much can make fine print unreadable. Keep the original images when they are the authoritative records.","For a batch of receipts, place each image on its own page and check that dates and totals remain legible at normal zoom.","Can different image sizes go into one PDF?","Yes, but each page may scale differently. Preview the full document and choose consistent page handling before sharing.","https://helpx.adobe.com/acrobat/using/creating-simple-pdfs-acrobat.html')"],
  ["how-to-extract-pages-from-pdf","How to Extract Pages from a PDF into a New File","Save selected PDF pages separately while preserving order and avoiding accidental disclosure.","pdf-page-extractor","Extracting pages creates a smaller document containing only a selected range. This is useful for sharing a chapter or a signed page, but it also creates a new copy that needs its own review. Identify pages using the PDF viewer’s displayed page numbers and confirm whether printed page labels differ.","Select the required pages and export them as a new PDF. Open the new file and verify page order, count, bookmarks, and links. Check for information in headers, footers, or attachments that should not accompany the extract. Use a descriptive filename and keep the source file unchanged.","Deleting or extracting visible pages does not necessarily remove hidden attachments or metadata from a source file. If privacy matters, inspect the exported copy with an appropriate document review process. Avoid sharing a whole PDF when only a few pages are needed.","Extract pages 5 through 7, then check that the new document has exactly three pages in the intended order.","Does extracting PDF pages change the original?","It should create a separate output when you choose extract or save as. Verify the source remains intact and inspect the new file.","https://helpx.adobe.com/acrobat/using/rotate-move-delete-crop-pdf-pages.html')"],
  ["how-to-convert-csv-to-excel","How to Convert CSV to Excel Without Losing Data","Import CSV into Excel with the right delimiter, encoding, and column types to preserve values.","csv-to-xlsx","CSV is plain text, so it does not carry spreadsheet formatting or explicit column types. Excel may interpret dates, long numbers, and identifiers automatically. Importing through a preview lets you choose delimiters and data types before opening the file, which can prevent leading zeros from disappearing.","Check the separator and text encoding, then import the CSV rather than double-clicking if type detection could change data. Set identifier columns such as postal codes to text, and inspect decimal and date columns. Save as XLSX after checking row counts, headers, and representative values.","Saving a CSV directly as a spreadsheet may silently change values such as 00123 into 123 or a long account number into scientific notation. Keep the raw CSV unchanged as a source and document any transformations made during import.","A product code column should be imported as text if values like 000472 identify a product; numeric formatting does not preserve that identifier reliably.","Why did Excel remove zeros from my CSV?","Excel inferred a numeric type. Reimport the column as text so leading zeros remain part of the value.","https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/import-or-export-text-txt-or-csv-files-5250ac4c-663c-47ce-937b-339e391393ba')"],
  ["how-to-remove-duplicate-rows-from-csv","How to Remove Duplicate Rows from a CSV Safely","Deduplicate CSV records using the right key and preserve the original file for comparison.","csv-deduplicate","Two rows that look alike may represent separate events, while records with different timestamps may still describe the same customer. Decide what makes a row a duplicate before removing anything: all fields, an email address, an order ID, or a combination of columns. Make a backup of the input.","Normalize only fields that should compare equally, such as trimming accidental surrounding spaces in an email column. Select the key columns and choose whether to keep the first or last matching row. Compare row counts and review a sample of removed records before accepting the output.","Deduplicating on every column will not catch records with small differences; deduplicating on one broad field can delete valid records. Do not lowercase or strip punctuation from values unless that matches the data owner’s rules. Keep an audit copy for reversible cleanup.","If order ID uniquely identifies a purchase, use that column as the key rather than the customer name, because one customer can have many legitimate orders.","Should duplicate rows be based on every CSV column?","It depends on the dataset. Define a reliable business key first, then review removed records before replacing the source.","https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4180')"],
  ["how-to-merge-csv-files","How to Merge CSV Files with Matching Columns","Combine CSV exports while checking headers, encodings, and duplicate records.","csv-merge","Merging files is straightforward only when their columns describe the same data in the same order and format. Exports from different systems may use different header names, separators, date formats, or encodings. Compare the schemas before appending rows, and decide whether to preserve a source filename column for traceability.","Inspect each header row and map equivalent columns to a common name. Confirm that field order and delimiters are consistent, then combine records and verify the expected total row count. Check samples from the beginning and end of each source file and validate dates, decimal separators, and non-English characters.","Appending mismatched columns can place values under the wrong heading without producing an obvious error. A merge does not automatically remove duplicate records or reconcile conflicting values. Retain the original exports and record the mapping used.","If three monthly exports each contain 500 data rows plus one header, expect 1,500 merged records and a single header row.","Can CSV files with different column orders be combined?","Yes, after mapping each file’s fields to the same named columns. Verify the output rather than relying on positional order.","https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4180')"],
  ["how-to-convert-json-to-excel","How to Convert JSON to Excel for Analysis","Flatten JSON records into spreadsheet columns and understand nested arrays and data loss.","json-to-xlsx","JSON can contain nested objects, arrays, nulls, and different fields on different records. A spreadsheet is a rectangular grid, so conversion requires a rule for representing those structures. Decide whether nested fields become dotted columns, repeated rows, or a separate sheet before exporting.","Use a small representative sample that includes nested and optional values. Convert it and inspect how arrays are expanded, how missing keys appear, and whether null differs from an empty cell. Confirm row counts and data types in Excel, especially long identifiers and timestamps.","Flattening can discard relationships when one object contains arrays of different lengths. Spreadsheet applications may also reinterpret dates or large numbers. Keep the source JSON and document the conversion convention so the export can be reproduced.","A customer record with several phone numbers may need repeated rows or a separate sheet; putting all numbers into one cell changes how the data can be filtered.","Does JSON to Excel preserve nested structure?","Not automatically. The converter must flatten or otherwise map nested values to a grid, so inspect arrays and optional fields carefully.","https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8259')"],
  ["how-to-validate-json-schema","How to Validate JSON Data Against a Schema","Use a JSON Schema to catch missing fields, wrong types, and invalid values before an import.","json-schema-validator","Valid JSON only confirms that the text follows JSON syntax. It does not show that required properties exist or that values fit an application’s contract. JSON Schema describes expected structure, types, constraints, and required fields so data can be checked before it reaches an API or database.","Start with the schema version expected by the receiving system. Validate a known-good sample, then add cases for a missing required property, an incorrect type, and an out-of-range value. Read the error path to find the failing field, and confirm that nested objects and arrays are validated as intended.","A schema can be syntactically valid but too permissive to catch important data problems. Conversely, strict additional-property rules can reject harmless extensions. Align the schema with the actual API contract and test changes before using it in production.","If quantity must be a positive integer, test zero, a negative number, and a decimal as well as a valid positive whole number.","Does JSON Schema validate the meaning of data?","It validates declared structure and constraints. Domain-specific meaning still needs explicit rules in the schema or application.","https://json-schema.org/learn/getting-started-step-by-step')"],
  ["how-to-create-a-qr-code-for-business","How to Create a QR Code for a Business Card or Flyer","Make a business QR code that scans reliably and points to the right destination.","qr-code-generator","A QR code can open a website, contact record, or other encoded text, but a scan is only useful when the destination works on a phone. Choose a stable URL and test it before printing. Decide whether the QR will be viewed on screen or printed small, since that affects its size and contrast.","Create the code with a short, tested destination and download it in a format suited to the design. Preserve a quiet margin around the code and use strong contrast. Scan the final artwork from another phone, then test the destination on mobile data and Wi-Fi before ordering print copies.","Do not place decorative graphics over the finder patterns or reduce the code until its modules blur. A QR code does not guarantee privacy; anyone who scans it can inspect the destination. If the URL may change, use a redirect you control and keep it maintained.","Print a draft at the intended size and scan it under ordinary room lighting before producing a large batch of flyers.","How do I make a QR code scan more reliably?","Use a clear, high-contrast code with a quiet border, preserve the finder patterns, and test the printed size with multiple phones.","https://www.qrcode.com/en/howto/')"],
  ["how-to-create-a-wifi-qr-code","How to Make a Wi-Fi QR Code for Guests","Generate a Wi-Fi QR code and test network name, password, and security settings before sharing.","wifi-qr-generator","A Wi-Fi QR code lets a compatible phone join a network without manually typing the password. The encoded details must match the network exactly, including capitalization and security type. Create a code only for a network you control and decide whether guest access should be separated from personal devices.","Confirm the network name, password, and authentication type in the router settings. Generate the code, then scan it from a device that is not already connected. Check that it offers the correct network and joins successfully. Place the printed code where guests can see it without exposing it publicly.","Anyone who can read the code may gain network access, so use a guest network with appropriate limits. Update or remove printed codes when credentials change. Do not post private network credentials in a public place or include them in an untrusted image.","Test the code using a guest phone before printing; a phone already connected may hide a typo because it never attempts a new join.","Is a Wi-Fi QR code safe to display?","It reveals access details to anyone who scans it. Use a restricted guest network and share the code only with intended visitors.","https://support.google.com/android/answer/9075847')"],
  ["how-to-create-an-email-signature","How to Create a Professional Email Signature","Build a readable email signature with contact details, links, and a compact mobile-friendly layout.","email-signature-generator","An email signature should make it easy to identify you and reach you. Keep it brief, use text for essential details, and avoid relying on an image that may be blocked by the recipient’s mail client. Decide which contact methods are appropriate for external messages and remove outdated titles or phone numbers.","Include your name, role, organization, and one or two useful contact links. Check that the links use complete HTTPS addresses and that the text remains readable on a phone. Send a test message to accounts using different mail clients and check replies and forwarded messages for excess signature repetition.","Large banners, multiple colors, legal disclaimers, and social icons can make a signature difficult to read or inflate message size. Follow your organization’s branding rules and avoid adding personal information that recipients do not need.","A short plain-text signature with a linked company site often survives replies more cleanly than a large image-based card.","Should an email signature be an image?","Keep important contact information as selectable text. Images may be blocked and are less accessible to screen readers.","https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/images/')"],
  ["how-to-write-a-follow-up-email","How to Write a Follow-Up Email That Gets a Clear Reply","Write a concise follow-up with context, a specific request, and a courteous next step.","email-writer","A useful follow-up reminds the reader what the earlier conversation was about and makes the next action easy to understand. Before writing, identify the original request, the response or decision needed, and any deadline that is genuinely relevant. One focused question is easier to answer than a list of unrelated requests.","Use a subject line that preserves context, open with a brief reference to the earlier message, and state the requested action in a direct sentence. Include a reasonable date only when there is a real timeline. Close politely and add relevant material inline rather than attaching duplicate files without explanation.","Repeated messages sent too quickly can feel intrusive and make it harder to find the original context. Avoid implying that someone agreed when they did not. If circumstances have changed, explain what is different instead of sending the same template again.","“Could you confirm whether Tuesday works for the review? If another day is better, please suggest one.” gives the recipient a concrete, easy response.","How long should I wait before following up?","It depends on the request and any stated deadline. Use the timing agreed in the conversation and keep the follow-up relevant and courteous.","https://owl.purdue.edu/owl/subject_specific_writing/professional_technical_writing/email_etiquette.html')"],
  ["how-to-write-a-resume-summary","How to Write a Resume Summary with Specific Evidence","Create a concise resume summary that connects relevant experience to the role without vague claims.","resume-bullet-generator","A resume summary should help a reader understand the role you fit and the evidence that supports it. Start from the job description and select two or three requirements that match your real experience. A broad statement such as “hard-working team player” uses space without showing what you have done.","Name your role or specialty, summarize relevant years or scope where accurate, and include a measurable result or concrete strength. Use terms from the posting naturally, then read the summary beside the experience section to confirm that the evidence is present. Tailor the emphasis for each application.","Do not claim a skill, credential, or result you cannot explain in an interview. Numbers need context: state the baseline, timeframe, or size when it changes how the result should be interpreted. Keep the summary short enough that important experience remains visible.","“Operations coordinator who reduced invoice processing time from five days to two across a 12-person team” gives a reader scope and evidence.","Should every resume have a summary?","No. Use one when it adds targeted context; omit it if it only repeats the job title or generic traits.","https://www.careeronestop.org/JobSearch/Resumes/ResumeGuide/ResumeGuide.aspx')"],
  ["how-to-write-product-descriptions","How to Write Product Descriptions That Answer Buyer Questions","Explain product benefits with accurate specifications, useful details, and scannable copy.","product-description-generator","A product description helps shoppers decide whether an item suits their needs. Start with verified product facts, common buyer questions, and any limitations that affect fit or use. Benefits are strongest when they connect a feature to a practical outcome without making claims the product cannot support.","Lead with the product’s purpose, then organize dimensions, materials, compatibility, care instructions, and included items so they are easy to scan. Use precise units and explain variants clearly. Compare every statement with the manufacturer’s specifications and make shipping or warranty terms easy to find elsewhere on the page.","Do not invent performance, health, environmental, or durability claims. Avoid keyword repetition that makes copy hard to read. If colors or sizes vary, describe the exact variant shown and make selection options obvious before purchase.","“Fits a 13-inch laptop with a 2 cm protective sleeve” is more useful than “perfect for every laptop.”","What should a good product description include?","Include accurate features, dimensions, compatibility, materials, what is included, and details that help a buyer judge fit.","https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/advertising-marketing-internet-rules-road')"],
  ["how-to-create-a-weekly-meal-plan","How to Create a Weekly Meal Plan and Grocery List","Plan flexible meals around your schedule, pantry, budget, and dietary requirements.","weekly-meal-plan","A practical meal plan reduces last-minute decisions and helps use ingredients before they spoil. Begin with the number of meals needed, time available for cooking, and food already in the pantry. Plan a few flexible components that can be reused rather than assigning an elaborate new recipe to every day.","Choose meals around shared ingredients, schedule leftovers intentionally, and write the grocery list by store section. Check package sizes and pantry quantities before shopping. Leave one flexible meal for a busy day or changing plans, and note any preparation that can be done safely ahead of time.","A plan is only useful if it fits the people eating it. Account for allergies and dietary needs, and follow safe storage and cooking guidance for perishable food. Do not buy ingredients in quantities that are unlikely to be used before they spoil.","Roast a tray of vegetables once, then use portions in a grain bowl and a soup on separate days to save preparation time.","How do I make a meal plan easier to follow?","Choose meals that fit your real schedule, reuse ingredients deliberately, and leave room for leftovers and unexpected changes.","https://www.myplate.gov/myplate-plan')"],
  ["how-to-calculate-sales-tax-on-a-purchase","How to Calculate Sales Tax on a Purchase","Calculate tax from a price and rate, and distinguish tax-inclusive totals from pre-tax prices.","sales-tax-calculator","Sales tax calculations depend on the taxable amount and the rules of the location where a purchase is made. For a simple estimate, multiply the taxable price by the rate as a decimal and add the result to the price. Real transactions may include exemptions, local rates, or special tax treatment.","Convert a rate such as 7.5% to 0.075, multiply by the taxable subtotal, and add the tax. If the displayed price already includes tax, divide the gross price by one plus the rate to estimate the pre-tax amount. Check rounding rules and whether shipping or discounts affect the taxable base.","A calculator cannot determine local legal taxability by itself. Rates and exemptions can change, and purchases may cross city or state boundaries. Use official tax guidance for filing, invoices, or a binding compliance decision.","At a 7.5% rate, a taxable $40 subtotal produces $3 in tax and a $43 total before any other charges.","Is sales tax calculated before or after a discount?","It depends on the applicable jurisdiction and transaction. Check official local rules for the taxable base.","https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/sales-tax')"],
  ["how-to-calculate-discount-percentage","How to Calculate a Discount and Final Sale Price","Find the savings and final price from a percentage discount, including sequential offers.","discount-calculator","A percentage discount reduces a price by a fraction of its original amount. Multiply the original price by the discount rate to find the savings, then subtract that amount. For multiple offers, check whether the seller applies them successively or adds the percentages together; those methods produce different totals.","Convert the percentage to a decimal, multiply by the original price, and subtract the result. For example, 20% off $80 saves $16 and leaves $64 before tax. If a second 10% discount applies to the reduced price, calculate it from $64 rather than assuming the total discount is 30%.","A sale label may exclude shipping, tax, or required fees. Confirm the checkout total and read any conditions such as minimum spend or selected products. Compare unit prices when package sizes differ, because the lowest sticker price may not be the best value.","A $100 item reduced by 20% and then 10% costs $72 before tax: $100 × 0.80 × 0.90.","Do two 20% discounts equal 40% off?","Usually not when applied one after another: the second reduction applies to the already discounted price.","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/')"],
  ["how-to-convert-annual-salary-to-hourly-pay","How to Convert Annual Salary to an Hourly Rate","Estimate an hourly equivalent from annual salary and scheduled work hours.","salary-to-hourly-calculator","An annual salary can be converted to an approximate hourly equivalent by dividing it by paid work hours in a year. The result depends on the hours and weeks assumed. It does not automatically account for unpaid leave, overtime eligibility, benefits, bonuses, or differences between scheduled and actual hours.","Multiply weekly hours by the number of paid weeks to estimate annual hours, then divide annual salary by that total. Use the employee’s actual schedule where possible. For a 40-hour schedule across 52 weeks, the denominator is 2,080 hours; adjust it for unpaid weeks or a different work pattern.","This calculation is a comparison aid, not a payroll determination. Local wage rules, overtime, paid leave, and benefits affect compensation. A contractor’s billable rate also needs to cover taxes, insurance, equipment, and non-billable time.","A $62,400 salary divided by 2,080 scheduled hours equals a simple hourly equivalent of $30, before benefits and other compensation.","Does salary divided by 2,080 show take-home hourly pay?","No. It is a gross-pay estimate and does not account for tax withholding, benefits, or hours not actually worked.","https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/flsa')"],
  ["how-to-calculate-overtime-pay","How to Estimate Overtime Pay from Hours and Rate","Estimate overtime earnings using regular hours, overtime hours, and an assumed pay multiplier.","overtime-pay-calculator","An overtime estimate begins with the regular hourly rate and hours worked in the relevant work period. Some rules use a premium such as one and a half times the regular rate, but eligibility and calculation methods depend on jurisdiction and job classification. Confirm the applicable policy before relying on an estimate.","Separate regular hours from hours that may qualify for overtime, then apply the correct multiplier to the eligible hours. Include different pay rates or compensation components only as required by the governing rules. Compare the estimate with an actual pay statement and note the workweek or pay period used.","Do not assume every worker or every extra hour qualifies under the same rule. Daily overtime, weekly overtime, exemptions, collective agreements, and local law can differ. Use official labor guidance or payroll support for a wage dispute or compliance decision.","If an eligible 5 overtime hours use a 1.5 multiplier on a $20 rate, the simple estimate is 5 × $30 = $150 gross overtime pay.","Is overtime always paid at 1.5 times the hourly rate?","No. The applicable rule depends on location, worker classification, and the type of hours worked.","https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/overtime')"],
  ["how-to-build-an-emergency-fund","How to Set an Emergency Fund Savings Target","Estimate a cash reserve from essential expenses and make a realistic monthly savings plan.","emergency-fund-calculator","An emergency fund is cash set aside for essential expenses during an unexpected interruption or cost. A useful target depends on monthly needs, income stability, dependants, insurance, and access to other resources. Start with essential bills rather than a generic percentage of income.","List housing, utilities, basic food, transport, minimum debt payments, and necessary care. Multiply the monthly total by a chosen number of months, then subtract any dedicated savings already available. Set an automatic contribution that fits the budget and review the target when household costs or circumstances change.","A savings target is not a guarantee against every emergency, and cash has an opportunity cost. Keep the reserve accessible and separate from routine spending, while considering deposit protections and fees. Avoid treating investments with volatile values as a guaranteed short-term reserve.","If essential costs total $2,200 monthly, a three-month planning target is $6,600 before subtracting current emergency savings.","How much should I keep in an emergency fund?","There is no single amount for everyone. Base the target on essential expenses, income stability, and your circumstances.","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/an-essential-guide-to-building-an-emergency-fund/')"],
  ["how-to-plan-a-trip-budget","How to Build a Travel Budget Before Booking","Estimate transport, lodging, food, local travel, and contingency costs before a trip.","trip-budget-calculator","A travel budget is more useful when it reflects the full itinerary rather than only flight and hotel prices. List fixed bookings separately from daily spending and include transport to the airport, meals, local transit, entry fees, connectivity, and a reserve for changes. Use current prices for the dates you plan to travel.","Enter costs in one currency, note which items are per person and which are per group, and multiply daily expenses by the number of travel days. Check cancellation terms and baggage fees. Keep a modest contingency line and recheck estimates when dates, exchange rates, or group size change.","Forecasts are not quotes, and foreign exchange rates and travel prices can move. Avoid counting a refundable deposit as a final expense twice. For international trips, confirm current entry rules, insurance coverage, and payment access with authoritative sources.","For a four-day trip, separate the one-time rail fare from daily meal and transit estimates so the total scales correctly if the stay changes.","What costs are easy to forget in a trip budget?","Include local transport, baggage, meals, fees, connectivity, exchange charges, and cancellation or contingency costs.","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/')"],
  ["how-to-calculate-fuel-cost-for-a-road-trip","How to Estimate Fuel Cost for a Road Trip","Estimate trip fuel use from distance, vehicle efficiency, and fuel price, then include uncertainty.","trip-fuel-cost-calculator","A basic road-trip fuel estimate uses distance, fuel consumption, and the price per unit. If your vehicle uses liters per 100 km, multiply the distance by that rate and divide by 100. Route changes, traffic, weather, vehicle load, and driving style can make the actual amount differ.","Use the planned route distance and a realistic consumption figure from recent driving. Multiply estimated fuel volume by the current price, and convert units consistently if distance and consumption use different systems. For a return trip, include both directions and likely detours rather than assuming the outbound distance is the total.","Fuel estimates do not include tolls, parking, charging alternatives, or price changes along the route. A vehicle’s advertised economy may differ from real-world use. Check the route and fuel availability before departure, especially in remote areas.","A 600 km route at 7 L/100 km uses about 42 liters; at €1.70 per liter, the simple fuel estimate is €71.40.","How accurate is a road-trip fuel estimate?","It is a planning estimate. Actual consumption and prices vary with route, traffic, weather, load, and driving conditions.","https://www.fueleconomy.gov/feg/driveHabits.jsp')"],
  ["how-to-calculate-flooring-for-a-room","How to Estimate Flooring for a Room","Measure floor area and add a practical allowance for cuts, pattern matching, and waste.","flooring-calculator","Flooring estimates start with the actual floor area, but the purchase quantity is usually higher because boards or tiles must be cut and some material may be damaged. Measure each room separately, including alcoves, and use the product’s package coverage rather than relying on a rough room label.","For a rectangular room, multiply length by width using the same unit. Break an irregular floor into rectangles and add their areas. Apply a waste allowance appropriate to the layout and material, then round up to full packages. Follow the manufacturer’s guidance for patterned layouts, diagonal installation, and expansion gaps.","An estimate does not replace an installer’s site measurement. Subfloor condition, transitions, closets, and future repair stock can affect the quantity. Keep a few matching pieces when practical, since dye lots and product lines may change.","A 4 m by 3.5 m room has 14 m² of floor area before adding the product-specific cutting allowance.","How much extra flooring should I buy?","It depends on material, room shape, pattern, and installer guidance. Use the manufacturer’s recommendation and verify measurements on site.","https://www.thisoldhouse.com/flooring')"],
  ["how-to-estimate-paint-for-a-room","How to Estimate How Much Paint a Room Needs","Estimate wall paint from room dimensions while accounting for coats, openings, and coverage labels.","paint-calculator","Paint quantity depends on the area to cover, number of coats, surface texture, and the coverage listed on the product. Measure walls rather than guessing from floor area. Include ceilings or trim separately if they use different paint, sheen, or color.","Add wall widths and multiply by wall height to get gross wall area. Subtract large doors and windows only when they materially affect the estimate. Divide by the label’s coverage per liter or gallon, then multiply by the number of coats. Buy from one batch when color consistency matters.","Coverage on a can is an estimate for suitable surfaces; porous, textured, or previously unpainted walls can require more. Primer may be needed for stains, repairs, or color changes. Follow product directions and ventilation guidance, and check local disposal rules for leftover paint.","A 4 m by 3 m room has 14 m of wall perimeter; at 2.5 m height, gross wall area is 35 m² before openings.","Does paint coverage include two coats?","Usually the label gives coverage per coat. Multiply the area-based quantity by the number of coats you plan to apply.","https://www.sherwin-williams.com/homeowners/how-to/paint-calculator')"],
  ["how-to-calculate-concrete-for-a-slab","How to Estimate Concrete for a Slab","Calculate slab volume from length, width, and thickness, then plan for practical ordering needs.","concrete-calculator","Concrete is ordered by volume, so convert the slab’s dimensions into consistent units before multiplying. A small thickness error across a large slab can change the quantity considerably. This estimate is a planning aid; base preparation, reinforcement, drainage, and structural design require appropriate project guidance.","Measure length and width and convert thickness to the same unit. Multiply the three dimensions to find volume, then convert cubic units to the supplier’s ordering unit. Add only the allowance recommended for the project and supplier. For multiple shapes, calculate each section and add the volumes.","Do not use a calculator to decide structural thickness or reinforcement for a load-bearing project. Uneven ground, forms, waste, and delivery minimums affect the final order. Ask the concrete supplier or qualified contractor to confirm quantities and placement requirements.","A 4 m × 3 m slab at 0.10 m thickness is 1.2 cubic meters before any ordering allowance.","How much extra concrete should I order?","It depends on site variation and delivery practice. Have the measured volume reviewed by the supplier or contractor before ordering.","https://www.cement.org/learn/concrete-technology/concrete-construction')"],
  ["how-to-calculate-running-pace","How to Calculate Running Pace from Distance and Time","Convert elapsed time and distance into pace per kilometer or mile and compare training splits.","running-pace-calculator","Running pace expresses elapsed time per unit distance, such as minutes per kilometer or mile. Divide the total time by distance, using consistent units. The average pace is useful for planning, but it can hide hills, stops, and changes in speed during a route.","Record moving time and the measured distance, then select kilometers or miles. Convert the result into minutes and seconds per unit. For interval training, calculate each split separately and compare it with the average. GPS distance can drift near tall buildings or under tree cover, so treat small differences cautiously.","Pace is not the same as speed, and a watch’s instantaneous reading can fluctuate. Use a measured track or route when precision matters. Increase training gradually and account for conditions; a pace calculator is not medical or coaching advice.","Running 5 km in 30 minutes gives an average pace of 6 minutes per kilometer.","How do I convert minutes per mile to minutes per kilometer?","Convert distance units or use a pace converter; remember that minutes per unit are not converted like ordinary decimal numbers.","https://www.runnersworld.com/training/a20806124/how-to-calculate-your-running-pace/')"],
  ["how-to-calculate-age-in-days","How to Calculate Your Age in Days Between Two Dates","Find elapsed calendar days between birth and a chosen date, including leap years and date conventions.","date-difference-calculator","Age in days is the elapsed number of calendar days between a birth date and a comparison date. The answer depends on whether the starting day is counted and which time zone defines each date. For ordinary date-only calculations, use calendar dates rather than dividing years by 365.","Enter the birth date and the target date using the same calendar and locale. Check whether the calculator reports elapsed days or inclusive counting. Test a known date interval that crosses February 29 if the range includes a leap year. For timestamps, account for time zones and daylight-saving changes.","A date-only result is different from hours lived because birth time and local time-zone shifts matter. Do not use a general age calculator for legal age cutoffs without checking the jurisdiction’s exact rule.","From March 1 to March 2 is one elapsed calendar day; inclusive counting would count both named dates and produce a different total.","Does a year always contain 365 days for age calculations?","No. Leap years add a calendar day, so count actual dates across the interval.","https://www.timeanddate.com/date/duration.html')"],
  ["how-to-plan-a-moving-checklist","How to Make a Moving Checklist by Date","Organize a move into timed tasks for utilities, packing, address changes, and handover.","checklist-generator","A moving checklist works best when tasks are tied to deadlines and ownership. Start from the move date and work backward: booking, notice periods, address changes, utilities, packing, cleaning, and key handover may each have separate lead times. Keep confirmation numbers and contact details together.","Group tasks into weeks and mark dependencies, such as measuring furniture before arranging transport. Label boxes by room and essentials, photograph meter readings, and confirm access and parking with both addresses. Keep medication, documents, chargers, and first-night items in a separate bag that stays with you.","Requirements vary by lease, location, and provider. Confirm notice terms, service dates, and local disposal rules rather than relying on a generic checklist. Avoid putting valuables or identity documents into boxes handled by a moving crew.","One week before the move, confirm the booking, update service dates, and pack rarely used items; leave daily essentials accessible.","When should I start a moving checklist?","Start as soon as the move date is known, then confirm deadlines with landlords, utilities, and service providers.","https://www.usa.gov/change-address')"],
  ["how-to-make-a-plant-watering-schedule","How to Make a Plant Watering Schedule That Fits Conditions","Build a watering reminder around plant type, soil dryness, season, and pot drainage.","plant-watering-schedule","A fixed watering calendar can overwater one plant and underwater another. Water needs depend on plant species, light, temperature, pot size, soil mix, and drainage. Use a schedule as a reminder to inspect the soil, not as an instruction to water regardless of conditions.","Group plants with similar needs, note their light and pot setup, and check moisture using a method appropriate to the plant. Water thoroughly when the top layer or root zone reaches the plant’s preferred dryness, then allow excess water to drain. Reassess more often during heat or active growth.","Yellow leaves can have several causes, including excess water, poor drainage, pests, or low light. Do not let pots sit in standing water unless the species specifically requires it. Research species-specific care from a reliable horticultural source.","A weekly reminder can prompt a soil check; if the soil is still moist, postpone watering and review drainage rather than following the calendar blindly.","Should I water every plant once a week?","No. Use plant and soil conditions to decide. A calendar should remind you to check, not force the same watering frequency.","https://extension.umn.edu/houseplants/watering-houseplants')"],
  ["how-to-create-a-pdf-from-a-webpage","How to Save a Webpage as a Clean PDF","Print a webpage to PDF and check page breaks, headers, links, and content that may not print.","html-to-pdf","A webpage is designed for scrolling, while a PDF is paginated. Browser print settings determine how content flows across pages and whether backgrounds, headers, links, or interactive content appear. Decide whether you need a readable archive, a form record, or a print-ready document before choosing settings.","Use the browser’s print preview, select Save as PDF, and check margins, scale, and page orientation. Remove unnecessary navigation or background graphics when the page offers a reader view. Inspect page breaks around tables and headings, and verify that the saved file includes the date or URL if those matter for reference.","Web content can change, and a PDF may omit scripts, embedded media, or content loaded only after interaction. Respect copyright and privacy when archiving pages. For evidence or compliance records, follow the organization’s required capture process rather than relying on a casual printout.","Preview a long article before saving; a heading stranded at the bottom of one page may make the PDF harder to read.","Does saving a webpage to PDF preserve interactive features?","Usually not. It captures printable page content, so forms, animations, and embedded media may be omitted or become static.","https://support.google.com/chrome/answer/1069693"],
] as const;

